# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Armat — local environment
#
# Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in the two values from your Supabase
# project (Project Settings → API). `.env.local` is git-ignored.
#
# Supabase is used for ONE purpose: storing newsletter email addresses. All
# educational content lives in local TypeScript files under src/data.
#
# Both variables are NEXT_PUBLIC_ on purpose — the anon key is meant to be
# public and is protected by row level security, not by secrecy.
#
# NEVER put the service_role key in this file or in any NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable.
# It bypasses row level security and would be shipped to every visitor.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Contact form — SMTP
#
# Used ONLY by the server route /api/contact. None of these carry the
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, so they never reach the browser bundle — that is the
# whole reason the contact form posts to a server route instead of talking to
# a mail service directly. Do not add the prefix to any of them.
#
# SMTP_SECURE=true for implicit TLS on port 465; leave it false for 587, which
# upgrades via STARTTLS. If your provider gave you a dedicated app password,
# use that as SMTP_PASSWORD rather than the account's own password.
#
# CONTACT_FROM_EMAIL must be an address the server is allowed to send as — it
# defaults to SMTP_USER. The reader's own address is set as Reply-To, never as
# the sender, because that would fail SPF and land the message in spam.
# CONTACT_TO_EMAIL is where messages arrive; it defaults to the site's
# published contact address.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_SECURE=false
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASSWORD=
CONTACT_FROM_EMAIL=
CONTACT_TO_EMAIL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Visit map — basemap tiles
#
# Optional. Leave all three blank and the map uses its development fallback:
# the public OpenStreetMap tile endpoint. That endpoint is an external
# community service run by the OpenStreetMap Foundation — not infrastructure
# Armat operates or has an agreement with — and production should be pointed
# at an appropriate provider before meaningful traffic. No availability,
# throughput or coverage guarantee is made here about any provider, including
# that one.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_URL         Leaflet URL template for the raster tiles.
#                                  Must contain {z}, {x} and {y}.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_ATTRIBUTION Copyright line rendered on the map. HTML is
#                                  allowed. Required by every tile licence
#                                  worth using, so it is required here too.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_MAX_ZOOM    Highest zoom level the provider serves.
#                                  Defaults to 17.
#
# All three are NEXT_PUBLIC_ on purpose. A tile URL is visible in every request
# the browser makes and the attribution is printed on the map by design, so
# neither is a secret and neither can be. The same holds for a provider's
# browser token if one is ever needed: it belongs in the URL above and is
# public in exactly the same way. Nothing that must stay private — see the SMTP
# block — may ever take the NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix.
#
# Configure the URL and the attribution together or not at all. Setting one
# without the other, or a template missing a placeholder, is refused rather
# than patched over: the map is not drawn, a notice says so, and the list of
# places below it is unaffected. Serving one provider's tiles under another
# provider's copyright line is the one failure a licence actually punishes.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_ATTRIBUTION=
NEXT_PUBLIC_MAP_TILE_MAX_ZOOM=
